## Requirements

* macOS with the Xcode command line tools: `xcode-select --install`
* GNU Make (ships with the command line tools)

## Build

```sh
make            # {% if is_gui %}-> {{ class_name }}.app in build/{% else %}-> build/{{ bin_name }}{% endif %}

make run        # {% if is_gui %}build and open the .app bundle{% else %}build and run{% endif %}

{% if is_console %}
make test       # build and run the unit tests
{% else %}
make codesign   # ad-hoc sign the bundle
make install    # copy the bundle into /Applications
{% endif %}
make clean
```

MakeMaker can drive the build for you:

```sh
makemaker build .
makemaker run .
```

## Layout

```
{{ slug }}/
├── Makefile              build driver
{% if is_console %}
├── CMakeLists.txt        optional CMake build
├── src/
│   ├── app.h             shared declarations
│   ├── app.{{ source_ext }}             application core
│   └── main.{{ source_ext }}            entry point
└── tests/
    └── test_app.{{ source_ext }}        unit tests (make test)
{% else %}
├── Info.plist            bundle metadata
└── src/
    ├── app.h             shared declarations
    ├── app.m             application core
    └── gui.m             AppKit window + entry point
{% endif %}
```
{% if is_gui %}

The GUI flavour is Objective-C with ARC and builds a real `.app` bundle, so it
runs without opening Xcode. To move to Xcode later, create a new macOS App
project and drop `src/` and `Info.plist` into it.
{% endif %}
